You can use Skribisto entirely from the keyboard, with a screen reader such as NVDA, JAWS,
Orca or VoiceOver. This page describes the window and the keys that move around it.

## How the window is laid out

A project window has four areas:

- *In the centre*, the items you have opened, each in a tab of its own. The centre can show
  two items side by side.
- *On the left*, the panels about the whole project: *Binder*, *Search*, *Trash*,
  *Comments* and *Writing games*. One of them shows at a time.
- *On the right*, the panels about the item in front of you: *Inspector*, *Format*,
  *This document*, *Footnotes* and *Versions*.
- *At the bottom*, *Preview* and *Go back in time*. They stay hidden until you open them.

`F6` jumps from one area to the next, and `Shift+F6` to the one before. Your screen reader
says where you arrive: *Left panel*, *Main area* or *Right panel*.

`Tab` moves through the window in reading order: the title bar, the left, the centre, the
right and the status bar. The text you write keeps `Tab` for itself, to indent a list or
insert a tab: to leave it, press `Ctrl+Tab`, and `Ctrl+Shift+Tab` to go back the other way.
A synopsis, an epigraph, a comment and a footnote are fields like the others, and `Tab`
leaves them.

## Open a panel with its shortcut

Each panel has its own shortcut, which brings it to the front and puts you in it:

- On the left: `Ctrl+Shift+B` *Binder*, `Ctrl+Shift+F` *Search*, `Ctrl+Shift+T` *Trash*,
  `Ctrl+Shift+C` *Comments* and `Ctrl+Shift+G` *Writing games*.
- On the right: `Ctrl+Shift+I` *Inspector*, `Ctrl+Shift+O` *Format*, `Ctrl+Shift+D`
  *This document*, `Ctrl+Shift+K` *Footnotes* and `Ctrl+Shift+R` *Versions*.
- At the bottom: `Ctrl+Shift+J` *Preview* and `Ctrl+Shift+L` *Go back in time*.

*View ▸ Panels* lists the same panels with their shortcuts, and `F9` shows or hides the
whole left side.

## Find your way in the binder

The binder is a tree of your chapters, scenes and notes. Your screen reader says each item's
level and its place among its neighbours. The Up and Down arrows go from item to item. The
Right arrow opens a folder, and the Left arrow closes it.

- `Enter` opens the item and puts the cursor in its text.
- `Ctrl+Enter` opens it in the other half of the centre.
- `F2` renames it, and `Delete` moves it to the trash.
- `Alt+↑` and `Alt+↓` move it up or down. `Ctrl+]` and `Ctrl+[` move it one level in or out.
- `Shift+F10` or the Menu key opens its menu, with every command for the item and, at the
  end, *Move*.

## Change the view of a chapter, part or book

A chapter, a part or a book opens with a bar of views at the top: its own page, the full
text, the synopses, the corkboard and an overview. A screen reader announces the bar as a
group of radio buttons named *Views*.

Use the arrows to change the view, and `Home` or `End` for the first and the last.

## Commands, menus and settings

- `F10` or `Alt` opens the menu bar. Your screen reader reads each command's shortcut after
  its name.
- `Ctrl+Shift+P` opens the command palette. Type part of a command's name, then press
  `Enter`.
- `Ctrl+G`, or *Go ▸ Go to…*, finds an item by its name and opens it.
- While you write, `Alt+↓` and `Alt+↑` go to the next and the previous item and put the
  cursor in its text.
- In a date field, `F4` or `Alt+↓` opens the calendar. Move to a day with the arrows, then
  press `Enter`, or `Escape` to leave the date as it was. `Tab` stays inside the calendar,
  which also has *Cancel* and *OK*.
- `Ctrl+,` opens *Settings*. In the list of pages on the left, use the arrows, then press
  `Enter` to go to the page's first setting.
- In *Settings*, the lists of the *Tags*, *Statuses*, *Templates*, *Personal dictionary* and
  *Text replacements* pages read each line by what it holds. `F2` or `Enter` goes into a
  line, `Tab` moves between its fields, and `Escape` comes back to the list.
- `F1` opens this help. `Enter` on a topic takes you into its text. In a page, `Tab` goes
  from link to link, and `Enter` follows one.

## Where to take care

Two things do not work well yet:

- On a braille display, a page of this help arrives one line of the screen at a time, as if
  each line were a paragraph. The text you write arrives a paragraph at a time.
- A screen reader does not yet announce the headings of a help page as headings, so you
  cannot jump from one to the next.
